<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaliningrad Oblast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> Oblast is a Russian exclave on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, centred on the city of Kaliningrad, formerly Königsberg. It occupies the northern part of the former German province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, home in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to Old Prussian peoples such as the Sambians and Natangians, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> conquered in the 13th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed the territory after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and it was added to the Russian SFSR in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast.jpg" alt="Kaliningrad Oblast" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RaviC at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kaliningrad_Oblast.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its borders changed repeatedly: Turkestan was divided in 1924, the Kazakh and Kirghiz autonomous republics became separate Union republics in 1936, Tuva was annexed in 1944, the <b>Kaliningrad</b> region was added in 1946, and Crimea was transferred to Ukraine in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Nadruvia lay in the north-east of Prussia, south of the Neman, in what is now the <b>Kaliningrad</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/nadruvians">Nadruvians</a></li><li><blockquote>Natangia lay south of the Pregolya river in central Prussia, divided today between Russia's <b>Kaliningrad</b> region and Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/natangians">Natangians</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sambians lived on the Sambian peninsula between the Vistula and Curonian lagoons, in today's <b>Kaliningrad</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/sambians">Sambians</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scalovians, or Skalvians, lived on both banks of the lower Neman in what is now the <b>Kaliningrad</b> region of Russia and western Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/scalovians">Scalovians</a></li></ul></section>
